From: Arthur build <arthur@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: nss: delete VAPs before NSS teardown on crash recovery

Seen on IPQ6018 (JDCloud RE-SS-01) with NSS offload on 2026-11-11: after a
firmware crash (SSR) wireless clients got no traffic through ECM-accelerated
flows, and the 2.4 GHz TCL ring stayed full (tx_desc_in_use 512/512,
tx_hw_enqueue_fail climbing) until reboot.

ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() calls ath11k_nss_teardown(), which only
stops wifili, deinits each pdev (releasing the radio dynamic interface) and
resets NSS. The per-vif NSS VAPs are never deleted. Recovery then halts each
radio, which only unlinks arvif from ar->arvifs, and ieee80211_restart_hw()
calls ath11k_mac_op_add_interface() for every interface. That clears arvif,
so arvif->nss.created is false, and a second VAP is allocated and registered
for the same netdev. The old VAP (if_num 31 on the device) is leaked and
stays registered for phy0-ap0; nss_cmn_get_interface_number_by_dev() returns
the first match, so ECM keeps sending accelerated traffic to a VAP bound to
the destroyed radio, where it is dropped.

Delete the VAPs before the teardown, the same order an unload uses
(interface removal before ath11k_core_deinit()): per radio, under
conf_mutex, send VAP down and delete it (unregister, dealloc, clear
created). This must run while ab->nss.enabled is still true and
ar->nss.ctx is still valid, which ath11k_nss_pdev_deinit() clears.

Locking: conf_mutex is taken and released per radio before core_lock is
taken, never nested with it, in the same worker that takes conf_mutex
right after in ath11k_core_post_reconfigure_recovery(). The NSS vdev
calls here are the ones mac.c already makes under conf_mutex.

The reset path (ath11k_core_reset(), e.g. simulate_fw_crash hw-restart)
halts the radios before ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() runs, emptying
ar->arvifs, so the deletion is also done there before the halt.

Not covered: AP_VLAN ext vdevs (WDS / dynamic VLAN) and mesh VAPs.
ath11k_info() logs how many VAPs were deleted.

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
index 5eb80d8..6e58f2d 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
@@ -2515,12 +2515,61 @@ err_firmware_stop:
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* Recovery re-adds every interface through ath11k_mac_op_add_interface(),
+ * which clears arvif (and so arvif->nss.created) and allocates and registers
+ * a new NSS VAP for the same netdev. ath11k_nss_teardown() releases only the
+ * radio interfaces, so without this the old VAP stays allocated and
+ * registered for the netdev, and is found first by
+ * nss_cmn_get_interface_number_by_dev(): ECM keeps accelerating flows to a
+ * VAP bound to the destroyed radio and they are dropped. Delete the VAPs
+ * while NSS is still enabled and ar->nss.ctx is still valid, i.e. before the
+ * teardown, the way interface removal does it before an unload.
+ *
+ * Takes conf_mutex per radio, never nested with core_lock.
+ */
+static void ath11k_core_nss_vdevs_delete(struct ath11k_base *ab)
+{
+#ifdef CPTCFG_ATH11K_NSS_SUPPORT
+	struct ath11k_vif *arvif;
+	struct ath11k *ar;
+	int i, ret, deleted = 0;
+
+	if (!ab->nss.enabled)
+		return;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ab->num_radios; i++) {
+		ar = ab->pdevs[i].ar;
+		if (!ar)
+			continue;
+
+		mutex_lock(&ar->conf_mutex);
+		list_for_each_entry(arvif, &ar->arvifs, list) {
+			if (!arvif->nss.created)
+				continue;
+
+			ret = ath11k_nss_vdev_down(arvif);
+			if (ret)
+				ath11k_warn(ab, "failed to down nss vap %d for recovery: %d\n",
+					    arvif->nss.if_num, ret);
+
+			ath11k_nss_vdev_delete(arvif);
+			deleted++;
+		}
+		mutex_unlock(&ar->conf_mutex);
+	}
+
+	ath11k_info(ab, "nss: deleted %d vap(s) for recovery\n", deleted);
+#endif
+}
+
 static int ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash(struct ath11k_base *ab)
 {
 	int ret;
 
 	ath11k_hif_irq_disable(ab);
 
+	ath11k_core_nss_vdevs_delete(ab);
+
 	mutex_lock(&ab->core_lock);
 
 	ath11k_nss_teardown(ab);
@@ -2795,6 +2844,11 @@ static void ath11k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 	ath11k_coredump_collect(ab);
 	ath11k_core_pre_reconfigure_recovery(ab);
 
+	/* The reset path halts the radios (emptying ar->arvifs) before
+	 * ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() runs, so delete the VAPs here.
+	 */
+	ath11k_core_nss_vdevs_delete(ab);
+
 	reinit_completion(&ab->reconfigure_complete);
 	ath11k_core_post_reconfigure_recovery(ab);
 
